# Appendix E — Existing-Account Diagnostic & Audit Plan

> **Scope:** Snapshot-based diagnostic plan for ErgoWorks Consulting, not a completed Google Ads audit. It uses verified account data as at 12 July 2026, including last-30-day search terms, the full keyword/status/match-type/headline-Quality-Score inventory and 493 negatives. A full audit still requires longer history, RSAs/assets, complete campaign settings, Quality Score components and geo/device performance reports. No live-account changes are authorised by this plan.

## E1. Account baseline and audit boundaries

This is an optimisation plan for a mature Search program, not a greenfield launch.

| Item | Verified baseline |
|---|---|
| Google Ads account | **9258098368** — “zzz Ergoworks” |
| GA4 property | **316175981** — **ergoworksconsulting.com.au** |
| Focus campaign | **700072710** — Ergoworks Consulting Search |
| Current bidding | **Maximize Conversions**, with **no target CPA** and no portfolio strategy |
| Tracking foundations | Auto-tagging on; GA4-linked conversions |
| Trailing-12-month scale | Approximately **A$40,000/year** |
| Observed monthly planning range | Approximately **A$3.0k–A$4.7k/month** |
| Last 30 days | **A$4,246 spend; 4,540 impressions; 407 clicks; 26 reported conversions** |
| Estimated real lead volume | Approximately **20/month** |
| Indicative real-lead CPA | **A$4,246 ÷ 20 ≈ A$212 per lead** |
| Account contamination | 16 enabled conversion actions, including Physiotherapy actions outside Consulting scope |
| Missing growth layers | No paid Meta activity; no dedicated conversion landing page; paused PMax and remarketing |
| Evidence limitation | Search terms (30-day), keyword/match-type/status/headline-Quality-Score inventory, 493 negatives and a partial geo signal were pulled 2026-07-12 (`research/live-evidence-2026-07-12.md`, `research/live-keywords-negatives-2026-07-12.md`); still missing: longer performance history, negative-list audit, RSA/asset inventory, complete campaign settings, Quality Score components and geo/device performance reports |

The 26 reported conversions are not equivalent to 26 genuine Consulting leads:

| Last-30-day conversion action | Reported conversions | Proposed role |
|---|---:|---|
| Calls from ads | 10 | Secondary native proxy; the qualified offline import is the only Primary call signal |
| Make an Enquiry | 10 | Primary, subject to validation and spam/internal exclusions |
| Newsletter Sign Up | 4 | Secondary |
| Email Click | 1 | Secondary |
| Call Forwarding | 1 | Secondary |
| Other enabled actions, including Physiotherapy actions | Not attributed in the campaign snapshot | Remove from Consulting campaign goals or retain only in the correct business scope |

## E2. First priority: repair the conversion objective

No bidding or budget decision should precede conversion cleanup. Campaign 700072710 currently uses Maximize Conversions, so every primary action can influence bidding. Counting newsletter registrations and soft engagement actions as success risks directing spend toward users most likely to take easy actions rather than become qualified Consulting opportunities.

### Proposed Consulting conversion hierarchy

| Conversion stage | Definition | Google Ads treatment |
|---|---|---|
| Qualified call | Unique Consulting-attributed call that meets the client-approved duration and operating-hours conditions and is dispositioned as a legitimate new-business B2B workplace-ergonomics enquiry; exact rule awaiting client approval | **Primary only through the qualified offline import once reliable** |
| Qualified lead | Enquiry or call accepted in the CRM after confirming a business need, organisational fit, valid contact and plausible service scope | Secondary while validated; future Primary after sufficient clean offline volume |
| Proposal issued | Qualified opportunity that reaches a documented proposal stage | Secondary initially; eligible for value-based optimisation later |
| Closed-won | Signed Consulting engagement with verified revenue or margin | Secondary initially; future value signal once volume and data quality are sufficient |
| Newsletter Sign Up | Marketing subscription only | **Secondary** |
| Email Click | Click on an email link without confirmed enquiry | **Secondary** |
| Click-to-call | Click or tap without a connected, qualified conversation | **Secondary** |
| Call Forwarding | Technical call-routing interaction without qualification | **Secondary** |
| Local actions | Directions, local engagement or other soft action | **Secondary** |
| Snapshot request (`snapshot_request`) | Valid B2B request for the free 15-minute Workplace Ergonomics Risk Snapshot | Primary initially |
| Direct enquiry (`direct_enquiry`) | Valid B2B direct-enquiry form submission | Primary initially |

Wherever offered, the **free 15-minute Workplace Ergonomics Risk Snapshot must be described as preliminary guidance, not a compliance assessment, medical assessment, diagnosis or certification**.

### Qualified-call implementation rule

The final rule must be identical to the definition adopted in Appendix C. Until that rule is approved, use this proposed implementation hook:

> **Awaiting client approval:** a qualified call is a unique inbound call attributable to ErgoWorks Consulting that reaches a human or approved call-handling service, meets the approved minimum connected-duration and operating-hours conditions, and is dispositioned as a legitimate B2B workplace-ergonomics enquiry. It must exclude spam, wrong numbers, job seekers, suppliers, consumer equipment-shopping enquiries, Physiotherapy, clinical or personal-health enquiries, and repeat calls about the same opportunity.

Duration is a screening condition, not proof of qualification. The CRM/call-tracking disposition is authoritative. The exact duration and operating-hours rule remains **awaiting client approval**. Keep any native duration-based proxy **Secondary**. Import `qualified_call` as the only Primary call signal using a stable order ID derived from the CRM opportunity/call record. If a proxy was previously imported in error, retract or restate it with Google Ads conversion adjustments before the qualified import; never count the proxy and qualified import as two conversions for the same call.

### Required conversion-cleanup sequence

1. Inventory all **16 enabled conversion actions**, their source, category, counting method, attribution, lookback window, campaign-goal inclusion and owning business.
2. Define a campaign-specific Consulting goal set for campaign 700072710.
3. Remove Physiotherapy conversion actions from the Consulting campaign’s optimisation scope. Do not apply account-wide exclusions or negatives that could damage the separate Physiotherapy campaign.
4. Demote Newsletter Sign Up, Email Click, Call Forwarding, clicks-to-call and local/soft actions from primary to secondary.
5. Validate Make an Enquiry against actual form submissions and exclude spam, tests and duplicates.
6. Implement the qualified-call rule and document how call disposition is recorded.
7. Add offline imports for qualified lead, proposal and closed-won stages using stable click identifiers and CRM records.
8. Reconcile Google Ads, GA4 and CRM totals before treating the new baseline as clean.
9. Preserve historical annotations so the reporting discontinuity is visible.

Changing primary conversions will change Smart Bidding behaviour and reported CPA. Expect a learning and restabilisation period; do not interpret an immediate fall in reported conversions as lost business when easy actions have deliberately been removed.

### Measurement and privacy gate

Before new forms, remarketing audiences or server-side signals go live:

- Keep enquiry consent separate from optional marketing consent.
- Do not collect injury details, symptoms, diagnoses or other health information in advertising forms.
- Implement an appropriate CMP and **Consent Mode v2, default denied, with no client- or server-side analytics/advertising transmission before consent**.
- Complete an Australian privacy review covering GA4, GTM, CRM, Google Ads, Meta Pixel and CAPI.
- Scope tags, streams and containers so Consulting data does not leak into ErgoEquip or Physiotherapy datasets.
- Use only controllable-output claims; do not claim guaranteed injury prevention, assured WHS compliance or quantified claims reductions.
- Treat awards, client logos, Danone, “ASX 100” and “5.0/63 reviews” as **client sign-off required** before any paid-media use.

## E3. Bidding assessment and decision gate

### Current position

Campaign 700072710 is verified as using **Maximize Conversions with no target CPA**. It produced 26 reported conversions in the last 30 days, but only approximately 20 appear to be real leads. The current optimisation signal is polluted by four Newsletter Sign Ups plus soft actions.

The required order is therefore:

1. Clean conversion goals.
2. Validate qualified-lead capture and import.
3. Allow the campaign to restabilise.
4. Compare bidding strategies using clean outcomes.
5. Consider tCPA or tROAS only after sufficient volume and stability.

### Rubric conflict

The prescribed ads-budget rubric says:

- Below 30 conversions/month: use **Maximize Clicks with a CPC cap**.
- Between 30 and 50 conversions/month: use **Maximize Conversions**.
- Use tCPA only after stable CPA and greater conversion volume.

The synthesis, however, concludes that **Maximize Conversions is the practical ceiling at approximately 20 real conversions/month**. The live campaign also has established Search history rather than being a new campaign.

This conflict should not be resolved by automatically switching a mature campaign to Maximize Clicks. Maximize Clicks optimises traffic volume rather than lead quality and could amplify irrelevant demand before the resolved 30-day search-term evidence and existing 493-negative inventory have been fully audited and extended with longer history.

### Recommended assessment

| Condition after cleanup | Bidding recommendation |
|---|---|
| Fewer than 20 clean primary conversions/month, weak or unstable delivery, and no reliable qualified-lead imports | Test whether Maximize Clicks with a benchmark-based CPC cap can rebuild usable volume, but only after search terms and negatives are reviewed |
| Approximately 20–29 clean conversions/month with stable spend, acceptable query quality and consistent qualified-lead rate | Retain Maximize Conversions as the incumbent ceiling and monitor; document the variance from the rubric |
| At least 30 clean conversions/month with stable CPA over multiple conversion cycles | Maximize Conversions is supported by the rubric; begin assessing tCPA readiness |
| At least 30 clean conversions/month but volatile lead quality or incomplete offline imports | Remain on Maximize Conversions; do not introduce tCPA yet |
| More than 50 clean conversions/month with stable qualified-lead CPA | Consider tCPA |
| Reliable revenue or margin values plus sufficient closed-won volume | Assess tROAS; conversion count alone is insufficient |
| Conversion goals have just changed | Hold major bid and budget changes until behaviour restabilises and conversion lag has been reviewed |

A CPC cap cannot be responsibly prescribed from the snapshot because keyword-level CPC/performance history and longer-term query distributions were not supplied, although match types and headline Quality Scores are resolved. It should be based on the extended keyword and search-term performance export, not the generic Search benchmark.

**Do not use ECPC.** It is deprecated and inappropriate here.

## E4. Spend concentration and conditional rebalancing

Manual Handling Training consumed **A$2,069**, or **49% of the A$4,246 last-30-day spend**. This concentration is directionally plausible because “manual handling training” is the dominant verified demand term at approximately 2,700 Australian searches per month. Concentration alone is not proof of waste.

Ergonomic Office Consulting generated seven reported conversions from A$719 spend, giving the best observed reported CPA:

\[
CPA = \text{A\$}719 \div 7 \approx \text{A\$}103
\]

That result is promising, but it is not yet a scale directive because the snapshot does not show whether those conversions were genuine enquiries, qualified calls, newsletter registrations or other soft actions.

### Conditional 3× Kill Rule

The 3× Kill Rule requires a business-approved target CPA. No target CPA or qualified-lead CAC ceiling has been supplied, so no ad group should be labelled for immediate pause from this snapshot.

Let **T** equal the approved clean target CPA.

| Diagnostic result | Minimum evidence | Conditional decision |
|---|---|---|
| Clean CPA is at or below T | At least 14 days, reconciled primary conversions and sufficient clicks | Maintain; assess incremental capacity |
| Clean CPA is above T but below 3T | At least 14 days and at least 20 clicks | Diagnose search terms, match types, ads, landing page and lead quality before changing budget |
| Clean CPA exceeds 3T | At least 7 days and at least 20 clicks | Flag for pause or restructure; do not pause until conversion quality and lag are checked |
| No clean conversions | At least A$100 spend or 50 clicks | Flag for diagnosis and possible pause |
| Reported CPA appears efficient but qualified-lead rate is poor | CRM dispositions available | Do not scale; repair targeting, message match or qualification |
| Reported CPA appears weak but qualified-lead or proposal rate is strong | CRM dispositions and downstream value available | Preserve while evaluating qualified-lead CPA and pipeline value |

For Manual Handling Training specifically, the diagnostic must separate query quality and lead quality before reallocation. Its large spend share may reflect genuine market demand, loose matching, weak negatives or some combination of the three. The 2026-07-12 keyword pull adds one refinement: `manual handling` PHRASE at QS=3 is in the rubric's **fail tier**, while several QS=5 keywords are in its **warning tier**. Diagnose the Quality Score components rather than treating all core keywords alike. Certificate intent also contains two cohorts: clearly individual leakage and a converting corporate/online slice ("manual handling certificate online australia" produced 1.33 reported conversions from A$15.92). **Do not blanket-exclude** certificate intent; use Appendix B's complementary-controls decision table and Appendix A's client-sign-off-required certificate block.

Separately, the "DSA - Ergoworks Consulting" ad group spent **A$795 in the last 30 days (18.7% of spend, 55 clicks, 4.5 reported conversions)**. Conversion quality for that group total is unverified. The live keyword pull shows it is not a Dynamic Search Ads group: it holds nine static EXACT keywords—six competitor-keyword entries across four competitors plus three generics. Only the sampled government/competitor-query subset was near-zero converting. Its evidence gap is therefore not "DSA settings and final URLs" but whether the group-level 4.5 reported conversions represent qualified demand and whether the sampled leakage warrants tighter query controls (see Appendix B reclassification).

### Conditional 20% Scaling Rule

Scaling is permitted only after a segment meets its clean target CPA consistently for at least two weeks and shows stable lead quality. The skill rubric also uses at least 50 conversions per week as a full “green light” for scaling; ErgoWorks is far below that threshold at approximately 20 real leads per month. This account therefore does **not** qualify for unconditional scaling under that rule.

| Condition | Decision |
|---|---|
| CPA at or below T for at least two weeks, stable qualified-lead rate, no material conversion lag and evidence of lost impression share to budget | Increase budget by no more than **20%**, then monitor for 3–5 days |
| CPA at or below T but impression share is constrained by rank | Improve relevance, ads, landing page and Quality Score before adding budget |
| CPA at or below T but conversion volume is fewer than 50 per week | Treat any increase as a controlled test, not a scale declaration |
| Budget increased by 20% and clean CPA deteriorates materially | Hold or reverse after accounting for conversion lag |
| Conversion definitions have recently changed | Do not scale until a comparable clean baseline exists |

The approximate A$103 reported CPA for Ergonomic Office Consulting makes it the first segment to validate for controlled expansion—not an automatic recipient of more spend.

## E5. Brand ad-group zero-delivery diagnosis


